WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.235 --> 00:00:08.773
On October 4, 1997,

00:00:08.806 --> 00:00:13.577
I walked into a room
unlike any room I'd ever entered before.

00:00:14.945 --> 00:00:18.516
People were standing up,
sharing their stories.

00:00:20.117 --> 00:00:22.987
A wave of relief washed over me.

00:00:26.123 --> 00:00:27.792
I got a cup of coffee

00:00:29.193 --> 00:00:31.028
and just took everything in.

00:00:33.431 --> 00:00:35.232
At that point,

00:00:35.266 --> 00:00:37.535
I was at the lowest point in my life.

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:42.139
I was out of ideas,

00:00:42.173 --> 00:00:45.776
and I didn't know how I was going
to make it out of the condition I was in.

00:00:48.279 --> 00:00:51.248
Before that date,
my life had not been easy.

00:00:52.883 --> 00:00:56.153
When I was 25 years old,
I gave birth to a son.

00:00:57.254 --> 00:00:59.123
We called him K.K.

00:01:02.426 --> 00:01:05.162
The next thing that happened
changed my life forever.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:08.099
He was struck by a car.

00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:11.368
That is the day I lost him.

00:01:12.736 --> 00:01:14.572
At only five years old,

00:01:15.506 --> 00:01:16.841
he was gone.

00:01:19.677 --> 00:01:24.482
His laughter, his love, his energy

00:01:24.515 --> 00:01:26.083
were all taken from me.

00:01:31.388 --> 00:01:33.757
My life became a downward spiral.

00:01:37.128 --> 00:01:38.529
I began to drink.

00:01:39.997 --> 00:01:41.999
Drinking led to substance use.

00:01:43.701 --> 00:01:46.637
For the substance use, I was imprisoned.

00:01:50.307 --> 00:01:52.209
Every time I left prison,

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:54.712
I swore I wouldn't go back.

00:01:55.746 --> 00:02:01.318
But I did. Over and over and over again.

00:02:02.119 --> 00:02:05.990
I remember one day,
standing in front of a judge

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:09.693
and asking that judge,
"Is there any help for me?"

00:02:11.962 --> 00:02:14.965
And the judge sentenced me
to prison one more time.

00:02:18.068 --> 00:02:19.537
No one cared.

00:02:20.704 --> 00:02:22.139
No one was listening.

00:02:23.707 --> 00:02:27.011
But when I walked into that room in 1997,

00:02:28.045 --> 00:02:30.781
I was ready to be heard.

00:02:30.781 --> 00:02:34.118
In the rooms of Alcoholics Anonymous,

00:02:34.118 --> 00:02:35.419
they listened.

00:02:38.656 --> 00:02:40.991
I took a deep breath and stood up,

00:02:42.359 --> 00:02:45.796
and said, "Look what drugs and alcohol
have done to me."

00:02:48.265 --> 00:02:51.168
The immediate compassion,

00:02:51.202 --> 00:02:53.337
empathy and love

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:56.974
that rolled off these complete strangers

00:02:57.007 --> 00:03:00.344
was enough to put a sizable dent

00:03:00.377 --> 00:03:03.981
in my pain, my shame,

00:03:04.014 --> 00:03:07.551
my guilt and all that sorrow.

00:03:09.954 --> 00:03:12.656
I kept talking,

00:03:12.656 --> 00:03:14.658
and they kept listening.

00:03:15.659 --> 00:03:18.596
I shared all the thoughts

00:03:18.629 --> 00:03:21.599
I had spent decades trying to silence.

00:03:23.534 --> 00:03:26.704
As they listened, I began to heal.

00:03:27.972 --> 00:03:31.875
Listening saved my life,
and it opened a door.

00:03:33.277 --> 00:03:35.379
I got sober in Santa Monica,

00:03:36.513 --> 00:03:38.949
an affluent part of Los Angeles.

00:03:40.551 --> 00:03:42.453
And I noticed something there.

00:03:43.754 --> 00:03:45.489
People in Santa Monica

00:03:46.757 --> 00:03:50.127
weren’t going to prison
for substance use,

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.762
for alcoholism.

00:03:53.464 --> 00:03:54.698
And I thought,

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.637
why didn't that judge
give me a paper and say,

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:00.904
"Miss Burton,

00:04:02.206 --> 00:04:03.741
go to AA.”

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:08.379
Was I not worth a piece of paper?

00:04:09.747 --> 00:04:13.384
Was I not worth
direction toward a solution?

00:04:15.219 --> 00:04:17.821
So as I got stronger in my recovery,

00:04:18.956 --> 00:04:21.592
I thought about women just like me,

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:25.195
cycling in and out of prison,

00:04:26.864 --> 00:04:29.266
looking for safety and not finding it.

00:04:30.434 --> 00:04:31.902
So I got a job.

00:04:33.871 --> 00:04:35.706
A minimum wage job.

00:04:37.341 --> 00:04:39.743
And I saved $13,000.

00:04:41.545 --> 00:04:44.848
And I took $3,000 of it
and put down on a house.

00:04:47.184 --> 00:04:48.752
And I bought some furniture.

00:04:50.487 --> 00:04:51.855
Every morning I’d wake up,

00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:54.024
and I'd go down
to the Greyhound bus station

00:04:54.058 --> 00:04:56.193
where women leaving prison
were dropped off.

00:04:57.528 --> 00:05:00.364
And I knew that station well

00:05:00.364 --> 00:05:04.168
because I had been dropped off there
so many times myself.

00:05:05.002 --> 00:05:08.539
And as they got off the bus
carrying a trash bag of belongings

00:05:09.373 --> 00:05:11.075
and hope in their hearts,

00:05:12.509 --> 00:05:15.512
I would walk up to them and I'd say,
"Girl, I have a house.

00:05:17.081 --> 00:05:19.049
And if you want to come live there,

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:21.819
it's a safe place to be.

00:05:22.786 --> 00:05:24.154
You can come live there."

00:05:25.489 --> 00:05:29.693
And before long there were 11 of us
living in that house.

00:05:30.861 --> 00:05:32.863
We cooked together.

00:05:32.863 --> 00:05:35.299
We went to meetings together.

00:05:35.332 --> 00:05:37.434
We shared resources.

00:05:38.535 --> 00:05:40.404
We laughed together.

00:05:40.404 --> 00:05:41.739
We cried together.

00:05:42.706 --> 00:05:46.009
But most of all, we healed together.

00:05:47.611 --> 00:05:51.115
Before long, people saw what I was doing,
and they joined me.

00:05:52.249 --> 00:05:54.084
And we created a team.

00:05:55.285 --> 00:05:58.622
Today, there [are] 12 houses
across Los Angeles.

00:06:00.391 --> 00:06:02.059
(Applause)

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:04.862
Thank you.

00:06:07.998 --> 00:06:11.902
What we built was a house
of women helping women.

00:06:13.971 --> 00:06:17.674
Each house is staffed
to support its residents

00:06:19.276 --> 00:06:21.678
who pay rent,

00:06:21.712 --> 00:06:23.847
do chores,

00:06:23.881 --> 00:06:25.883
buy groceries

00:06:25.916 --> 00:06:29.553
and learn to be accountable,
responsible members of the community.

00:06:31.321 --> 00:06:33.056
We help them find jobs,

00:06:34.658 --> 00:06:36.827
learn financial literacy

00:06:37.761 --> 00:06:39.997
and help them with any legal issues.

00:06:41.598 --> 00:06:46.003
As a result, thousands of women
have come back to the community

00:06:46.904 --> 00:06:51.875
and hundreds of mothers
have been reunited with their children.

00:06:53.544 --> 00:06:56.447
In 2017, I published my memoir,

00:06:57.214 --> 00:06:59.383
"Becoming Ms. Burton:

00:06:59.416 --> 00:07:01.685
From Prison to Recovery

00:07:01.718 --> 00:07:04.321
to Leading the Fight
for Incarcerated Women."

00:07:05.622 --> 00:07:10.427
In order to tell my story,
I held book talks all over the nation,

00:07:11.295 --> 00:07:12.930
behind prison walls.

00:07:14.131 --> 00:07:17.301
There I would listen to women.

00:07:18.569 --> 00:07:22.005
They were pleading for a place to go
after they were released.

00:07:23.073 --> 00:07:26.276
They were sitting with tears
streaming down their faces,

00:07:27.845 --> 00:07:30.614
wanting to rebuild their lives,

00:07:30.614 --> 00:07:33.517
be reunited with their children,

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:35.619
find a job in their community [and]

00:07:37.254 --> 00:07:39.823
break free from
the criminal justice system.

00:07:41.959 --> 00:07:43.794
Every year in this country,

00:07:45.295 --> 00:07:48.799
around two million women
are released from prisons and jails.

00:07:50.267 --> 00:07:54.104
They walk out with
a trash bag of belongings,

00:07:54.137 --> 00:07:56.773
hoping someone will listen.

00:07:56.807 --> 00:07:58.542
Hoping someone will help.

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:02.045
When there's no help,

00:08:02.079 --> 00:08:05.048
when there's no safe place to land,

00:08:05.082 --> 00:08:07.584
too many of them will go back.

00:08:08.819 --> 00:08:15.359
I knew I could not provide resources
of a new way of life

00:08:15.392 --> 00:08:16.960
to all those women.

00:08:18.862 --> 00:08:21.465
So I created SAFE,

00:08:21.498 --> 00:08:24.401
Sisterhood Alliance
for Freedom and Equality,

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:28.005
to replicate my model.

00:08:28.906 --> 00:08:32.009
Our first training was with 13 women.

00:08:34.077 --> 00:08:36.713
To date, we've trained over 700.

00:08:36.747 --> 00:08:38.782
(Applause)

00:08:43.287 --> 00:08:45.255
To listen.

00:08:45.289 --> 00:08:47.591
To create support.

00:08:47.624 --> 00:08:48.892
To give hope.

00:08:50.794 --> 00:08:55.799
When women like me have safe housing,

00:08:55.832 --> 00:08:58.135
families reunify,

00:08:58.168 --> 00:09:00.504
recidivism drops

00:09:00.537 --> 00:09:02.806
and communities stabilize.

00:09:04.541 --> 00:09:08.111
I started with one house
of women helping women.

00:09:09.246 --> 00:09:14.184
Today, there is an entire network
of women helping women

00:09:14.217 --> 00:09:17.721
in 20 US states and also in Uganda,

00:09:18.655 --> 00:09:21.592
Kenya and Nigeria.

00:09:21.625 --> 00:09:23.694
(Applause)

00:09:28.599 --> 00:09:31.969
We have replication down to a science.

00:09:33.136 --> 00:09:36.340
The leadership is there,
the demand is there,

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:39.543
and the goal is clear.

00:09:40.677 --> 00:09:43.513
More houses in more cities.

00:09:44.615 --> 00:09:48.919
More mothers reunited with their children.

00:09:48.952 --> 00:09:52.689
More women breaking a cycle

00:09:52.723 --> 00:09:54.925
that has lasted generations.

00:09:56.627 --> 00:10:01.298
I believe we all share a responsibility

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:07.838
to heal the harms caused
by the criminal justice system.

00:10:09.373 --> 00:10:11.575
This is public safety.

00:10:12.542 --> 00:10:15.045
This is family preservation.

00:10:15.779 --> 00:10:18.248
This is community repair.

00:10:19.449 --> 00:10:22.219
When someone listened to me

00:10:22.252 --> 00:10:26.189
with compassion rather than judgment,

00:10:26.223 --> 00:10:27.991
it changed my life.

00:10:29.693 --> 00:10:33.864
My story since that day in 1997,

00:10:35.365 --> 00:10:38.535
shows what is possible

00:10:38.535 --> 00:10:41.038
when people have support,

00:10:42.706 --> 00:10:44.574
hope

00:10:44.574 --> 00:10:46.209
and determination.

00:10:48.879 --> 00:10:50.847
I cannot bring my son back.

00:10:53.583 --> 00:10:58.422
But I know every time a woman
walks through the doors and finds safety,

00:11:00.757 --> 00:11:03.827
every time a child
is reunited with his mother,

00:11:05.696 --> 00:11:08.532
every time a woman breaks free

00:11:08.532 --> 00:11:10.834
from a cycle that once held me,

00:11:11.902 --> 00:11:15.205
his legacy goes way beyond his years.

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:19.776
I believe he's smiling down on my efforts,

00:11:21.244 --> 00:11:23.747
and I hope he's proud of his mother.

00:11:24.781 --> 00:11:26.516
I am so grateful

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:32.255
to be on the TED dot.

00:11:32.289 --> 00:11:34.524
(Laughter and Cheers)

00:11:34.558 --> 00:11:36.626
(Applause)

00:12:04.054 --> 00:12:09.526
And this happened because someone listened

00:12:09.559 --> 00:12:13.697
with compassion rather than judgment.

00:12:13.697 --> 00:12:15.098
Thank you so much.

00:12:15.132 --> 00:12:17.000
(Applause)

